  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 927 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nSUNDAY INTO MONDAY:\n\nA cold front pushing across eastern Utah and western Colorado \ntonight will usher in a cooler air mass, holding temperatures below \nthe seasonal averages to kick off the workweek. \n\nWidespread precipitation will continue across the region overnight \nbefore tapering off from southwest to northeast Monday afternoon and \nevening. An additional 0.5-1\" of rainfall remains possible across \nthe higher terrain of the San Juans and adjacent valley basin \nthrough early Monday. With snow levels dropping behind the front, \nsnow across the high passes will lead to slick and slushy travel \nduring the Monday morning commute. In the northern Divide mountains, \nensemble and deterministic guidance consistently depict 4 to 8 \ninches of accumulation above 9000 ft. through Monday evening; \nconsequently, the Winter Weather Advisory has been expanded \nnorthward to account for these localized travel impacts. \n\nHydrologic impacts will peak during the overnight hours into Monday \nmorning as area rivers approach crest levels. While precipitation \nchances diminish late Monday, rivers will remain near or above \nbankfull well after the rain ceases. Please refer to the hydrology \nsection below for specific river stage and crest forecast \ninformation. \n\nTHE REST OF THE WEEK:\n\nDrier conditions return Tuesday and temperatures will also continue \nto hover below normal for the better half of the work week. A quick \nmoving clipper system has the potential to bring some light showers \nto the region mid to late next week, although models don't have the \nbest grasp on this as of yet, so details remain vague.",
